  a few days that we had them with us but  we had to move a hundred horses 80  people and six gun carriages from  village in southeast London to a small  village in the West Country in England  and the the location Department turned  to huge fields into an army encampment  complete with grooms and stables and  tacking rooms and barracks and mess  rooms I mean it was incredible and those  guys were so brilliant with those horses  I mean well they were fantastic and  there was one time they were doing the  they did a circuit for the street scenes  and one of the riders fell off and we  were thinking nah and then he just got  back up and got on again and then  carried on nothing had happened  it means extraordinary Anna do you want  to tell us a little bit about your  process and your prep I mean I guess  that's the thing from having been on the  series is that I I've worked with the  characters in my head and worked with  the cast for a number of years already  so I had this really lovely shorthand  you know I could spot an Edith original  across a shop within 10 seconds which  meant that I was kind of already up to  speed and when it came on to prepping  the film and was then able to spend more  time considering our new characters and  sort of researching the sort of new  characters that came in and the and the  real-life depictions of the king and  queen so yeah it was it was a kind of  familiarity that was amazing but it was  also quite challenging because I used to  have nine episodes hundreds of costumes  per character for the upstairs to to  tell to tell a story  [Laughter]  to tell a story through through clothing  over over nine episodes is almost easier  than doing it with one film where I had  to be much more considered about what  clothes I put on each character because  it was a much more edited considered  version of the whole series there was  something about the the visit the  Queen's dress she was telling us about  earlier oh yeah so Queen Mary was an 