e  youngsters erm we we've we've seen them  grow up and we've come with them so he  that's why it went in that direction  without any doubt he just saw that  between us and he he made it work for  those characters and he did that with  everybody and I would say so Alan you're  the family fixer I think and you remind  me of another famous Tom in the movies  and that would be Robert Duvall's Tom  and the Godfather because you're sort of  the outsider in the family but you're  kind of on the outside but you're the  guy that gets it all done you yes how do  you feel about that I was delighted when  I got the the script I was very  surprised that Tom had so many elements  I mean he became dr. Phil to the royal  family which is like but I and all the  different elements and I was so happy to  see that love story begin to blossom  because it's something that I felt that  tongue really deserved at the end of  season tunics and he's for a long time  he's been on his own in that house so  and what I also love is the fact that  what you Lynne did so cleverly is he  create Tom's always been a victim of  circumstance he really didn't ask for  this life he fell in love with a girl  who sadly passed away due to contract  negotiations and and then so here Julian  took the braver choice to leave Tom  there and and but what I love is in Lucy  he's found someone who's a victim of  circumstance - and I love that idea on  how to say that scene at the very end  when they're dancing outside was never  scripted and it was completely the idea  of that lady there Liz Trubridge  romantic  who created it and our other producer  Gareth Neame said it'll never make the  movie and it proves him wrong and I most  certainly did and I love it because I  think it's lovely it's a lovely full  stop for his story as well do you still  take the underground around London and  are you not recognized I do  yeah I would say a lot of us still do I  do poetry totally um and you do get and  recognise the most enjoyable thing is  especially when the show used to be on  that someone will get on on a Monday  morning after it had been on and turned  to you and go hey have you been good  yeah great no I'm I don't know you do  you watch the TV and they go uh okay and  then they know where to get off at the  next stop because they feel a bit  awkward yeah well Elizabeth do you feel  like you have sort of infused Cora with  some uniquely American attributes well I  hope so I think that's inevitable given  the fact that I am American I'm playing  very hard to avoid I mean I tried from  the beginning to fight for my point of  view as an American to say but you know  I would do things differently I would  think about things differently I  wouldn't and I think and I did feel that  the script reflected that that often  Cora was able to be more flexible when  it comes to the traditional line on  issues and and I think that is the  benefit of the culture she comes from  and you you know Lord Grantham he's  still the lord of the manor and every  problem is still his problem is there  anything that you that wasn't on the  page that you felt like you brought to  Robert to help him sort of navigate all  of this I think I brought an immense  sense of being in retirement really but  well if you think about it you know  Mary's running the household even violet  says you know you're the next generation  I'm thinking what angle what about still  alive I'm still here I can but but but  that but that sense of handing on was  always there  you know particularly with Tom and Mary  as characters towards the end of the TV  show and that's that baton is still in  the process of being passed on but that  patriarchal figure I mean yes it's I  think it's delicious it's a bit like mr.  Bennet in in in Pride and Prejudice you  know that you know that there's this  whirlwind of women actually running  things and driving you know driving and  he just rather be in his study hiding  from it all but the key character let's  not forget is is the castle and without  getting to sort of philosophical and  sentimental it is a microcosm of the  world it will mr. Carson says at the end  in a hundred years time it will still be  here and the next generation will have  taken it on and our entire well I was  born my character was born to the duty  of preserving this place and passing it  on as best as possible as in the same  way we are on this planet to try and  pass it on as best as possible at the  moment we're not making a great job of  it let's face it and I think that again  is was something that the audience  relates to they identify with this sense  of place this this iconic building in  the middle of a landscape that has been  there for you know generations and will  be there for generations and we're all  passing through and each one of us knows  what it's like to be one of us I think  and I think that's again a key element  of the show what are you most proud of  in this film I would say each and every  member of not only the cast and crew  that have managed to take a very small  period drama in that I believe when we  started us as we all hoped it would do  well but the fact that we've taken that  through six seasons and now to the film  makes it it's such a sense of pride for  all of us because we are a family and we  are an ensemble in so many ways and for  me it's just it's the culmination of of  great friends great family great actors  great great technicians wonderful  artists so that's what I'm most proud of  I think I would say as an actor to be  allowed to be in something that has  truly touched people and made them  apparently this happy  is kind of what's why you joined in the  first place you know really I know  everybody gets the chance to have one of  these so I'm extremely proud to be to be  part of that sure I think I would I  would add to that that the responses  we've had not only to the TV show but  particularly now to the movie we've had  so many responses already saying it's  like being back with an old friend  and you know if you think back to when  the TV show for those who followed the  TV show it finished in 2015 the world  was a very very different place then  both in your country and ours and there  yes the show in itself by its very  nature has a sort of rose-tinted glow  about it it is about a different age but  it's also about a different age that  when we were watching it and we are in a  very peculiar state now certainly in our  country I won't speak for yours but we  are in a very peculiar place in our  country and our sense of national  identity is all over the place we don't  have a sense of national identity we  have families who are split down the  middle this the story that tone the  flavor of Downton you know whether it's  its social structure is right or wrong  it knew its structure it knew its  identity the people who would live and  work there know how they function how  they get through those that their life  in that house and I think there is a  great reassurance in that and I think  our timing as it was when when this TV  show came out is rather appropriate  people have said it's wonderful to  escape into something that is reassuring  for a couple of hours and that's what  our business is about thank you all  thank you thank this terrific group of  filmmakers

